# Resource Usage and Optimization Opportunities in Workflows of GitHub Actions

## Abstract

GitHub Actions is a popular continuous integration and continuous
delivery (CI/CD) platform integrated into GitHub. While prior research
has explored the adoption and evolution of GitHub Actions workflows,
this paper is the first to study the resource usage and optimization
opportunities of these workflows. We first collect and analyze a dataset
of 1.3 million workflow runs with 3.7 million jobs. We then analyze
workflow runs to study their resource usage in terms of runtime and
compute costs. We found that the median workflow run takes 6.5 minutes
and consumes 4.3 compute minutes, while 7.7% of runs exceed one hour and
6.3% exceed the free tier limit. Using this dataset, we also quantify
the potential impact of four optimization heuristics: deactivating
scheduled workflows after k consecutive failures, deactivating scheduled
workflows during repository inactivity, running previously failed jobs
first, and using project-specific timeouts. We estimate that these
optimizations could save up to 44% of compute time for scheduled runs
and reduce costs by up to $250 per repository per year. Our findings
provide actionable insights for developers and platform designers to
optimize GitHub Actions workflows.

## 1 Introduction

